PAPER 2 — THE FOUR GOSPELS

1. PURPOSE
Paper 2 aims at a scholarly understanding of the life of Jesus as contained in the four Gospels. Students need a working knowledge of the Gospel material and should be able to comment on significant differences between accounts.

2. WHAT IS A GOSPEL?
A Gospel is not simply a modern biography. The four canonical Gospels present Jesus through theological interpretation, narrative selection and proclamation. They contain historical traditions but are also shaped by the concerns and purposes of their authors and communities.

3. HISTORICAL, SOCIAL AND RELIGIOUS BACKGROUND
Students should understand:
• Jewish religious life;
• Roman imperial rule;
• Herodian political structures;
• synagogue life;
• temple significance;
• Pharisees and Sadducees;
• purity practices;
• taxation;
• social hierarchy;
• messianic expectations;
• Galilean and Judean settings.

4. BIBLICAL CRITICISM
Source criticism asks about written and oral sources behind the Gospels.
Form criticism studies recurring literary forms and their possible settings.
Redaction criticism examines how an evangelist has shaped inherited material.
Historical criticism asks about historical context, authorship and development.
These methods should be used carefully. They are tools for analysis rather than automatic proof that a passage is historically false or true.

5. AUTHORSHIP, DATE AND PROVENANCE
Students should know the traditional associations of Matthew, Mark, Luke and John while recognising scholarly debate about authorship and composition. The important examination skill is to explain how proposed dates, audiences and settings affect interpretation.

6. MARK
Mark is commonly regarded as the shortest Gospel and has a rapid narrative style. Themes include the identity of Jesus, discipleship, suffering, secrecy, conflict and the cross.
Students should examine how Mark presents the disciples, miracles, authority and Jesus' passion.

7. MATTHEW
Matthew strongly engages with Jewish Scripture and presents Jesus in relation to Israel's story. Themes include fulfilment, righteousness, discipleship, kingdom teaching and the church community.
The Sermon on the Mount is especially important for ethics and discipleship.

8. LUKE
Luke highlights themes such as salvation, the Holy Spirit, prayer, outsiders, women, the poor and God's concern for marginalised people. Luke's narrative also continues into Acts, making it important for understanding early Christianity.

9. JOHN
John has a distinctive style and theological vocabulary. Important features include:
• prologue;
• signs;
• “I am” sayings;
• long discourses;
• Nicodemus;
• Samaritan woman;
• farewell material;
• Paraclete/Spirit;
• distinctive Christology.

10. CHRISTOLOGICAL TITLES
Students should understand titles including:
• Son of God;
• Son of Man;
• Son of David;
• Messiah/Christ;
• Lord.
Each title should be interpreted in its literary and historical context.

11. MAJOR GOSPEL THEMES
• kingdom of God;
• salvation;
• forgiveness;
• discipleship;
• commitment;
• prayer;
• wealth;
• possessions;
• judgement;
• ethics;
• conflict with religious authorities;
• Jesus' self-understanding.

12. ZIMBABWEAN APPLICATION
Gospel themes can be applied to:
• poverty;
• inequality;
• forgiveness and reconciliation;
• youth leadership;
• integrity;
• treatment of outsiders;
• wealth and stewardship;
• church responsibility;
• social justice.

