PAULINE STUDY

1. PAUL'S THEOLOGICAL METHOD
Paul combines theology with pastoral problem-solving. His letters address real communities and therefore theology should be read in relation to practical circumstances.

2. ROMANS
Major themes:
• sin and human need;
• justification;
• faith;
• grace;
• law;
• Abraham;
• Israel and Gentiles;
• life in the Spirit;
• Christian ethics;
• relationship with governing authorities;
• unity.

Romans 1–3 develops the universal problem of sin.
Romans 4 uses Abraham in discussion of faith.
Romans 5–8 develops themes of Adam, Christ, grace, death, life and Spirit.
Romans 9–11 addresses Israel and God's purposes.
Romans 12–15 develops practical Christian ethics and community life.

3. JUSTIFICATION
Justification is a major Pauline concept. Students should avoid reducing it to a slogan. Paul is concerned with God's gracious action, human faith, the status of believers and the inclusion of Gentiles.
A strong answer should explain the relationship between faith, grace, law and transformed life.

4. GALATIANS
Galatians addresses the controversy over Gentile identity and circumcision.
Major themes:
• gospel;
• grace;
• faith;
• freedom;
• law;
• Spirit;
• Christian ethical responsibility.

Christian freedom is not presented as permission for selfish behaviour. Galatians connects freedom with love and life in the Spirit.

5. 1 CORINTHIANS
The letter addresses divisions and practical problems in Corinth.
Major issues include:
• church factions;
• wisdom;
• sexual ethics;
• lawsuits;
• marriage;
• food offered to idols;
• worship;
• spiritual gifts;
• resurrection;
• love.

1 Corinthians 12–14 is especially important for spiritual gifts and ordered worship. 1 Corinthians 13 places love at the centre of Christian community.

6. RESURRECTION
1 Corinthians 15 provides major teaching on resurrection. Paul connects Christian hope with Christ's resurrection and discusses transformation rather than merely continuation of ordinary earthly existence.

7. COLOSSIANS
Themes include Christ's supremacy, Christian identity, transformation, community relationships and ethical living.
Students should understand how Christology leads into practical ethics.

8. 1 THESSALONIANS
Themes include mission, suffering, Christian encouragement, holiness and expectation concerning the Lord's coming.
The letter demonstrates how eschatological hope can function pastorally.

9. PAUL AND SOCIAL LIFE
Paul's letters address real social structures but do not always provide modern political programmes. Application should identify principles such as equality in Christ, mutual responsibility, generosity, sexual integrity, work, leadership and community solidarity.

10. ZIMBABWEAN APPLICATION
Possible issues:
• church division;
• denominational competition;
• leadership disputes;
• poverty;
• sexual ethics;
• corruption;
• youth ministry;
• spiritual gifts and accountability;
• political authority;
• hope during economic hardship.

11. EVALUATION
Students should recognise that Pauline texts can be interpreted differently. A-level answers should identify the evidence for an interpretation and explain limitations rather than simply asserting a doctrinal conclusion.

